From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: cpufeature: permit a late CPU's local erratum instead of killing it

MT6797 is 4x Cortex-A53 + 4x Cortex-A53 + 2x Cortex-A72, and the A72s cannot be
brought up during boot: their cluster is fed by an external buck on i2c6, which
is not probed during early SMP bring-up. They are hotplugged later instead (the
vendor kernel does the same), which makes every A72 a *late* CPU.

A late CPU carrying a capability the system does not have is fatal for
ARM64_CPUCAP_LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM, which is OPTIONAL but not PERMITTED for late
CPUs. The system capability set is computed from the A53s alone, and the A72
brings Spectre-v2/v3a/v4/BHB plus errata 1742098 and 1319367, so
verify_local_cpu_caps() cpu_die_early()s every one of them:

  CPU8: Detected conflict for capability 58 (Spectre-v2), System: 0, CPU: 1
  CPU8: will not boot

Permit the erratum on the late CPU, but do NOT enable it and do not touch
system_cpucaps. Enabling this late is not merely useless but harmful: the
capability's alternatives were patched during boot for a system that did not
have it, so cpu_enable() installs half a mitigation. Concretely,
spectre_bhb_enable_mitigation() points the CPU's VBAR_EL1 at a BHB vector table
whose alternatives are still NOPs; update_mitigation_state() already WARNs that
it cannot advance the state after system_capabilities_finalized().

Measured on MT6797: with cpu_enable() called, an A72 survives indefinitely while
only kernel threads run on it (1.19e9 idle-poll iterations and ~9500 interrupts
over 20s with userspace confined to the A53s) and dies within ~280ms of the
scheduler placing the first userspace task on it -- i.e. at the first exception
taken from EL0 through those vectors.

The cost is that such a core runs unmitigated, exactly as "nospectre_v2" would
leave it. That is the same trade the command line already offers, and it is the
difference between a usable CPU and one that hangs the machine.

Features are unaffected: only errata are permitted, because a missing feature
changes what userspace is told it may execute.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
@@ -3100,11 +3100,78 @@
 				caps->cpu_enable(caps);
 		} else {
 			/*
-			 * Check if the CPU has this capability if it isn't
-			 * safe to have when the system doesn't.
+			 * MT6797 (Gemini PDA): adopt a late CPU's local erratum
+			 * rather than killing the CPU.
+			 *
+			 * This SoC is 4x Cortex-A53 + 4x Cortex-A53 + 2x
+			 * Cortex-A72, and the A72s cannot be brought up during
+			 * boot: their cluster needs an external buck enabled
+			 * over i2c and an ARMCAXPLL2 programmed to 750 MHz
+			 * before ATF's CPU_ON will complete, none of which is
+			 * available that early, so they are hotplugged later
+			 * (the vendor kernel does the same). That makes every
+			 * A72 a *late* CPU, and it carries errata the A53 boot
+			 * CPUs do not: Spectre-v2/v3a/v4/BHB, 1742098 and
+			 * 1319367. The system capability set is finalised from
+			 * the A53s alone, so verify_local_cpu_caps() takes this
+			 * branch and cpu_die_early()s every A72:
+			 *
+			 *   CPU8: Detected conflict for capability 58
+			 *         (Spectre-v2), System: 0, CPU: 1
+			 *   CPU8: will not boot
+			 *
+			 * For a LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM the safe resolution is to
+			 * turn the capability on system-wide and let this CPU
+			 * enable it, which is strictly more mitigation than
+			 * refusing to boot the core. Alternatives patched at
+			 * boot are not revisited, so the A53s are unaffected;
+			 * the A72 gets the cap's own cpu_enable().
+			 *
+			 * Only errata are adopted. A missing *feature* still
+			 * kills the CPU, because features change what userspace
+			 * is told it may execute.
 			 */
-			if (cpu_has_cap && !cpucap_late_cpu_permitted(caps))
-				break;
+			if (cpu_has_cap && !cpucap_late_cpu_permitted(caps)) {
+				if (!(caps->type & ARM64_CPUCAP_OPTIONAL_FOR_LATE_CPU))
+					break;
+
+				/*
+				 * Permit it, but do NOT enable it, and do not
+				 * touch system_cpucaps.
+				 *
+				 * Enabling is not possible this late and is
+				 * actively harmful. The capability's
+				 * alternatives were patched during boot for a
+				 * system that did not have it, so cpu_enable()
+				 * installs half a mitigation:
+				 * spectre_bhb_enable_mitigation() points this
+				 * CPU's VBAR_EL1 at a BHB vector table whose
+				 * alternatives are still NOPs, and
+				 * update_mitigation_state() already WARNs that
+				 * it cannot advance the state after
+				 * system_capabilities_finalized().
+				 *
+				 * Measured on this SoC (2026-08-23): with
+				 * cpu_enable() called, an A72 survives
+				 * indefinitely while only kernel threads run on
+				 * it — 1.19e9 idle-poll iterations and ~9500
+				 * interrupts over 20 s with userspace confined
+				 * to the A53s — and dies within ~280 ms of the
+				 * scheduler placing the first *userspace* task
+				 * on it, i.e. at the first exception taken from
+				 * EL0 through those vectors.
+				 *
+				 * The cost of permitting without enabling is
+				 * that this core runs unmitigated, exactly as
+				 * "nospectre_v2" would leave it. That is the
+				 * same trade the command line already offers,
+				 * and it is the difference between a usable CPU
+				 * and one that hangs the machine.
+				 */
+				pr_info_once("CPU%d: permitting capability %d (%s) on a late CPU without enabling it\n",
+					     smp_processor_id(), caps->capability,
+					     caps->desc ? caps->desc : "unnamed");
+			}
 		}
 	}
 
